 "lineas": [
  {
   "texto": "Why would anyone put a roof on a bridge?",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "In Lucerne, Switzerland, a wooden bridge with a roof has crossed the river since 1333.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Most people guess the roof is for the people walking across. It isn't.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "It's for the bridge.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Wood gets soaked every time it rains, then dries in the sun.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Wet, dry, wet, dry. Water sits in the joints, where the beams meet.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Fungus loves damp wood, and it eats the inside of the beam first.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "An open wooden bridge could rot through in ten or twenty years.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Replacing the whole thing meant cutting giant trees and closing the road.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "But a roof and side walls keep the rain off the beams that carry the load.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Now only the roof boards get wet, and those are cheap and easy to swap.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "A covered bridge can last eighty years, or more. Some stand for centuries.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Germany, Switzerland and the United States still have hundreds of them.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So the roof was never for you. It was armor for the wood. What should we explain next?",
   "pausa": 0.2
  }
 ],
